  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "s1",
      "short": "Two regions",
      "title": "Copies in two regions",
      "chapter": "setup",
      "body": "A ticket site keeps a copy of its data in Europe and in the US, so each region’s users get fast answers. Every write is replicated to the other side.\n\nOne concert has **1 seat left**, and both copies agree.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "insight",
        "text": "Replicating across regions buys speed and survival — and opens the question this lesson is about."
      },
      "changes": [],
      "metrics": {
        "consistent": "yes",
        "available": "yes",
        "errors": "0%",
        "conflicts": "0"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "short": "Partition",
      "title": "The network splits",
      "chapter": "problem",
      "body": "An undersea cable is cut. Both regions are healthy and their users can still reach them — but the regions can’t talk to *each other*. This is a [[network partition]].\n\nNow a US user wants to buy the last seat. The US copy can’t check with Europe. What should it do?",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "warning",
        "text": "Partitions aren’t rare: cables, routers, misconfigurations and overloaded links all cause them."
      },
      "changes": [
        "- Link between regions down"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "consistent": "?",
        "available": "?",
        "errors": "0%",
        "conflicts": "0"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "short": "Choose C",
      "title": "Option 1: stay consistent",
      "chapter": "technique",
      "body": "Refuse what can’t be confirmed. Europe holds the primary copy, so it keeps serving; the US side rejects writes — and reads that might be stale — until the link is back.\n\nNobody ever sees wrong data. But US users get errors for as long as the partition lasts.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Choose consistency where wrong answers cost money: payments, bank balances, inventory, ticket sales."
      },
      "changes": [
        "~ US side refuses requests",
        "- US users see errors"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "consistent": "yes",
        "available": "partly",
        "errors": "50%",
        "conflicts": "0"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "uss": {
          "visible": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s4",
      "short": "Choose A",
      "title": "Option 2: stay available",
      "chapter": "technique",
      "body": "Answer with what you have. Both sides keep accepting writes. Ana in Paris buys the last seat. Ben in Boston buys the last seat.\n\nEveryone gets an answer — and the two copies now **disagree**. When the link returns, there are two sales of one seat.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Choose availability where a brief disagreement is fine: likes, view counts, shopping carts, social feeds."
      },
      "changes": [
        "~ Both sides accept writes",
        "- Same seat sold twice"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "consistent": "no",
        "available": "yes",
        "errors": "0%",
        "conflicts": "1"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "uss": {
          "emphasis": true
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s5",
      "short": "Heal",
      "title": "Healing: eventual consistency",
      "chapter": "technique",
      "body": "The cable is repaired and the copies exchange what they missed. Left alone they will converge — [[eventual consistency]] — but someone has to decide what to do with the conflict.\n\nOptions: **last write wins** (simple, silently drops one sale), or let the app merge (keep both, refund one, apologise).",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "warning",
        "text": "“Last write wins” is the default in many databases. It loses data quietly — make sure that’s acceptable."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Link restored",
        "~ Copies exchange missed writes",
        "~ Conflict resolved: Ben refunded"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "consistent": "yes",
        "available": "yes",
        "errors": "0%",
        "conflicts": "0"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s6",
      "short": "Clocks",
      "title": "Case: “last” by whose clock?",
      "chapter": "problem",
      "body": "Last write wins needs to know which write *was* last — and each server stamps writes with its own clock. Clocks drift. Here the US server’s clock runs 3 seconds fast.\n\nA customer saves a delivery address through the US copy, then corrects it two seconds later through the EU copy. The correction is newer, but its timestamp is **smaller** — so when the copies sync, last write wins keeps the old address and silently throws the fix away.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Never order events across machines by wall-clock time. Use version numbers or **logical clocks** (Lamport timestamps, version vectors) that count events instead of reading the time — or keep both versions and let the app merge."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ US clock runs 3 s fast",
        "- The newer write carries the older timestamp",
        "- Last write wins keeps the wrong address"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "consistent": "yes",
        "available": "yes",
        "errors": "0%",
        "conflicts": "1"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s7",
      "short": "CAP",
      "title": "What CAP actually says",
      "chapter": "insight",
      "body": "The [[CAP theorem]]: when a network **P**artition happens, a replicated system must give up either **C**onsistency (every read sees the latest write, as if there were only one copy) or **A**vailability (every request to a working server gets an answer).\n\nYou don’t “pick two of three” — partitions will happen, so the real choice is what to do when they do.\n\nCAP’s “consistency” is what database people call **linearizability**. It is not the C in ACID, which is about a transaction keeping your data’s rules intact.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "insight",
        "text": "PACELC adds the everyday half: even without a partition, keeping copies in sync costs latency. Consistency is never free."
      },
      "changes": [],
      "metrics": {
        "consistent": "yes",
        "available": "yes",
        "errors": "0%",
        "conflicts": "0"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s8",
      "short": "Quorums",
      "title": "Quorums: tune the trade-off",
      "chapter": "technique",
      "body": "Many databases let you choose per request. With **N** = 3 copies, a write waits for **W** copies to confirm and a read asks **R** copies, using the newest answer.\n\nIf **W + R > N**, every read overlaps at least one copy that saw the latest write — here W = 2 and R = 2, so a read always reaches a `v2` even though one copy is stale.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "W = 1, R = 1: fast and available, possibly stale. W = 3: every copy must answer — consistent but fragile. Cassandra, Riak and ScyllaDB expose these knobs per request; DynamoDB offers the simpler choice of eventual or strongly consistent reads."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ 3 copies (N = 3)",
        "~ Writes wait for 2 (W = 2)",
        "~ Reads ask 2 (R = 2)"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "consistent": "yes",
        "available": "yes",
        "errors": "0%",
        "conflicts": "0"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s9",
      "short": "Per operation",
      "title": "Case: a payment and a like",
      "chapter": "technique",
      "body": "One app, two very different writes. A **like** can be confirmed by one copy and spread later (W = 1, R = 1): fast, always available, and nobody minds if a count is briefly off.\n\nRecording a **payment** (the big square) uses a majority for both the write and every read (W = 2, R = 2): slower, and it fails if two copies can’t be reached — rather than risk anyone reading a stale “unpaid”.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "You don’t pick consistency once for the whole system. Pick it per operation, by asking what a wrong answer would cost."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Likes: W = 1, R = 1",
        "+ Payments: W = 2, R = 2"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "consistent": "per op",
        "available": "per op",
        "errors": "0%",
        "conflicts": "0"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "c1": {
          "effect": "comet"
        },
        "c2": {
          "effect": "pulse"
        },
        "q3": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "c3": {
          "visible": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s10",
      "short": "Models",
      "title": "Consistency is a spectrum",
      "chapter": "insight",
      "body": "“Consistent” isn’t yes or no. Between the strongest ([[linearizable]]: everyone sees the latest write) and eventual sit useful promises: **read-your-writes** (you always see your own changes), **monotonic reads** (time never goes backwards for you), and **causal** consistency (replies never appear before the message they answer).\n\nWeaker promises are cheaper and more available. Pick the weakest one your users won’t notice.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "insight",
        "text": "Many “eventual” systems offer read-your-writes per session — enough for most apps."
      },
      "changes": [],
      "metrics": {
        "consistent": "per op",
        "available": "yes",
        "errors": "0%",
        "conflicts": "0"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "q3": {
          "emphasis": true
        },
        "c3": {
          "emphasis": true
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s11",
      "short": "Recap",
      "title": "Recap",
      "chapter": "recap",
      "body": "• A **partition** splits copies that can each still serve users.\n• During one, choose **consistency** (refuse) or **availability** (answer, maybe stale).\n• Available systems are **eventually consistent** and must resolve conflicts — without trusting wall clocks.\n• **Quorums** (W + R > N) tune the trade-off per request.\n• Pick the **weakest consistency** each operation can tolerate.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Next up: **Consensus & Leader Election** — how a group of servers agrees on one truth, even when some of them can’t be reached."
      },
      "changes": [],
      "metrics": {
        "consistent": "yes",
        "available": "yes",
        "errors": "0%",
        "conflicts": "0"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    }
  ]
